<article class="env-lemma" id="0BUU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BUU">Lemma <span data-tag="0BUU">37.22.8</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes which is flat and locally of finite presentation. Let $x \in X$ with image $s \in S$. Set $d = \dim _ x(X_ s)$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is Cohen-Macaulay at $x$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists an open neighbourhood $U \subset X$ of $x$ and a locally quasi-finite morphism $U \to \mathbf{A}^ d_ S$ over $S$ which is flat at $x$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
there exists an open neighbourhood $U \subset X$ of $x$ and a locally quasi-finite flat morphism $U \to \mathbf{A}^ d_ S$ over $S$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any $S$-morphism $g : U \to \mathbf{A}^ d_ S$ of an open neighbourhood $U \subset X$ of $x$ we have: $g$ is quasi-finite at $x$ $\Rightarrow $ $g$ is flat at $x$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Openness of flatness shows (2) and (3) are equivalent, see Theorem <a href="/tag/0399" data-tag="0399">37.15.1</a>. 
    <p>
      Choose affine open $U = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \subset X$ with $x \in U$ and $V = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) \subset S$ with $f(U) \subset V$. Then $R \to A$ is a flat ring map of finite presentation. Let $\mathfrak p \subset A$ be the prime ideal corresponding to $x$. After replacing $A$ by a principal localization we may assume there exists a quasi-finite map $R[x_1, \ldots , x_ d] \to A$, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00QE" data-tag="00QE">10.125.2</a>. Thus there exists at least one pair $(U, g)$ consisting of an open neighbourhood $U \subset X$ of $x$ and a locally<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000824144" id="a0000824144-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> quasi-finite morphism $g : U \to \mathbf{A}^ d_ S$. 
    <p>
      Claim: Given $R \to A$ flat and of finite presentation, a prime $\mathfrak p \subset A$ and $\varphi : R[x_1, \ldots , x_ d] \to A$ quasi-finite at $\mathfrak p$ we have: $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\varphi )$ is flat at $\mathfrak p$ if and only if $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ is Cohen-Macaulay at $\mathfrak p$. Namely, by Theorem <a href="/tag/039C" data-tag="039C">37.16.2</a> flatness may be checked on fibres. The same is true for being Cohen-Macaulay (as $A$ is already assumed flat over $R$). Thus the claim follows from Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00RE" data-tag="00RE">10.130.1</a>. 
    <p>
      The claim shows that (1) is equivalent to (4) and combined with the fact that we have constructed a suitable $(U, g)$ in the second paragraph, the claim also shows that (1) is equivalent to (2). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>